WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.373
Correct password hashing does not prevent operational exposure.

2
00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:16.164
Passwords and password-equivalent secrets can leak before hashing, during reset, through diagnostics, in support workflows, or from systems around the identity application.

3
00:00:16.164 --> 00:00:21.396
Those paths deserve review even when the database stores only password hashes.

4
00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:31.625
Never log plaintext passwords, password reset tokens, recovery codes, MFA secrets, raw request bodies from login forms, or credential-bearing headers.

5
00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:41.854
Review telemetry, exception reports, traces, support screenshots, analytics, crash dumps, request replay tools, browser storage, and test artifacts.

6
00:00:41.854 --> 00:00:48.804
If a tool captures requests or errors, decide how sensitive fields are suppressed before production use.

7
00:00:48.804 --> 00:00:54.192
Debug and development behavior should not silently become production behavior.

8
00:00:54.192 --> 00:01:06.608
Verbose error pages, request dumps, local logging habits, seeded accounts, and test shortcuts can become real exposure paths when copied into deployment scripts or shared environments.

9
00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:12.777
Production should have explicit redaction, least privilege, and monitoring expectations.

10
00:01:12.777 --> 00:01:15.978
Secrets used by the identity system also matter.

11
00:01:15.978 --> 00:01:29.331
Connection strings, signing keys, token-protection keys, email provider credentials, and data-protection keys should be stored outside source control and managed through approved secret storage.

12
00:01:29.331 --> 00:01:35.422
Access to identity databases, backups, logs, and admin tools should be limited and audited.

13
00:01:35.422 --> 00:01:40.575
Test data should not include real user passwords or realistic reused secrets.

14
00:01:40.575 --> 00:01:45.182
Support procedures should avoid asking users to disclose passwords.

15
00:01:45.182 --> 00:01:54.084
The safest password handling culture is one where teams do not need to see, copy, or store password material to operate the application.
